DISCLOSURE — dsh-bypass-web-token
=================================

What this package does
----------------------
`dsh-bypass-web-token` is a Cordis plugin for `dsh` (DeepSeek Harness). When
loaded into a dsh profile it overrides three methods on the live, in-process
`HostConnectionService` instance:

  * `admit`           — the gate used by `/api/*` HTTP routes and the
                        `/api/remote.mux` WebSocket upgrade;
  * `authorizeIndex`  — the gate that guards the web UI shell (`index.html`);
  * `authorizeConnect`— the `/connect` launch-token exchange.

With the overrides in place, requests that reach the dsh web listener are
admitted as one shared anonymous `owner` peer, and `/connect` answers with an
empty session cookie. Disposing the plugin restores the original methods by
reference, so the stock token/cookie authentication returns.

The package does not modify files on disk, does not fork or patch published
packages, does not register services, cron jobs, or shell hooks, and does not
persist anything across a dsh restart.

Why this is dual-use content
----------------------------
Any mechanism that removes an authentication gate can be used for good or for
harm. This package intentionally disables authentication on a developer tool's
local web surface, which automated scanning can reasonably read as an
authentication bypass. It is declared here so that npm can apply scanning
suited to dual-use packages.

Intended legitimate use
-----------------------
Local development and test automation on a machine the operator controls:
driving the dsh web UI with a browser automation tool, calling `/api/*` from
scripts without copying the printed `?token=` URL, and probing the WebSocket
mux from test harnesses. Typical deployments are a single-user workstation, a
disposable VM, a container, or a CI sandbox, bound to loopback or to a trusted
private LAN address.

Safety properties
-----------------
  * No install-time scripts: the package has no `preinstall`, `install`,
    `postinstall`, or `prepare` hooks.
  * No network activity of its own: no telemetry, no callbacks, no exfiltration.
  * No credential access: it never reads, logs, stores, or transmits tokens,
    cookies, API keys, or environment secrets.
  * Reversible and idempotent: every override keeps the exact original function
    reference and is restored on disposal; repeated installs share one patch
    through a reference count.
  * Fail-closed: a malformed service, a frozen instance, or any internal error
    degrades to HTTP 401 instead of opening access.
  * Process-local: nothing is written outside memory, so restarting dsh removes
    the effect entirely.

Explicit non-goals and warnings
-------------------------------
This is not an authentication scheme and must not be used in production or on
an internet-facing listener. While active, the peer it creates holds the
`owner` principal: any client that can reach the bound port — including other
processes on the machine and, on a LAN bind, other hosts on that network —
gets full read/write access to dsh sessions and RPC. The README repeats this
warning at the top.

